  "text_input": "A 74-year-old male presented with bilateral lower limb swelling for 2 months, extending to the mid-calf, non-painful, and increasing on walking. He also reported orthopnea (breathlessness while lying down) for 2 months. No palpitations, chest pain, abdominal distension, or fever were noted. He is a known hypertensive for 6 months on Telma-AM. History includes cervical spondylosis and early-stage Parkinson's disease. Physical examination showed BP 150/100 mmHg, pulse 90 bpm, and SpO2 95% on room air. Echo revealed EF 55% with sclerotic valves and normal systolic/diastolic function. He was diagnosed with amlodipine-induced pedal edema, systemic hypertension, subclinical hypothyroidism, and early-stage Parkinson's disease.",
